WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.767
-Our first guest tonight
is a very talented actress

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.501
and director.
She directs and stars in

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:05.868
"What Happens Later,"

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:07.434
which is in theaters
November 3rd.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:08.534
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:11.400
-Well, I always wondered
why you left.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.968
-No, you didn't.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.968
-We talked about it all
before I left Madison.

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:16.534
We sat in the back booth
at Morty's

00:00:16.534 --> 00:00:17.767
over our fries and beers,

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.701
and we very mature,
very civilized about it.

00:00:20.701 --> 00:00:22.601
Did a whole postmortem.

00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:24.267
Come on.
You know that's true.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.968
-No.

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:29.167
When people break up,
there's the thing

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:30.267
that they tell each other,

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:31.634
there's the thing
they tell their friends

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:34.234
and then --
then there's the truth.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.968
-Please welcome to the show.
Meg Ryan, everybody.

00:00:36.968 --> 00:00:39.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:46.501
♪♪

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:54.501
♪♪

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:57.367
I am so happy
to have you here.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.167
-Hi, Seth.
-This is a thrill for me.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.400
This is also very exciting.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.801
You don't just star
in this film.

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:04.000
You wrote
and directed this film.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.434
-Yeah.
-How did this story

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:06.868
first come to you?

00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:09.968
-Well, it was a play that the
playwright did a first draft of,

00:01:09.968 --> 00:01:14.234
and I -- it was during Covid,
and I had a lot of time,

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:16.367
and I just started writing,
and I kept writing

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.167
and I kept writing.
And at like 5:00 in the morning

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.501
I'd get more ideas
and more ideas.

00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:23.534
David Duchovny is in the movie
with me, too,

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:24.968
and he came on board.

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:28.767
We started, you know, sort of
circling the story together,

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:31.133
and suddenly we were financed.
And now here we are.

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:33.767
It was all like --
like happenstance, it felt like.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.133
-Did you -- when you
originally had the idea,

00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:38.000
you weren't planning
on playing the part?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.234
Is that correct?
-No, no.

00:01:39.234 --> 00:01:40.601
-And then once you were
playing the part,

00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:43.634
how important was it for you
to find a David Duchovny?

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:45.167
-There's just two people
in the movie.

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:46.300
-Yeah, well, there you go?
-That's it.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.467
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:50.067
So I had to have a partner,
and he is so,

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.033
you know,
there's just no one like him.

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:53.400
-He's really unique.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.100
He was here yesterday
and he's been here a bunch,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.734
and I really love him
because I just can't predict

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:01.067
what he's going to say.
-No, or how

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:02.234
he's going to say it.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:03.467
-Like, how he's going to say,
and I think

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:06.801
the clo-- the closest
comparable person

00:02:06.801 --> 00:02:09.334
is like Walter Matthau, I mean,
in the best possible way.

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:12.000
Like, he can spin a line,
he can break your heart,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.200
he can make you laugh.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.501
He -- and he's a great partner,
you know?

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.801
And really, we had each other
and he had a lot of patience.

00:02:17.801 --> 00:02:19.400
He took, like,
a big chance with me.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.033
I'm like a second time director
and he's, you know,

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:24.901
he's the person
we all know and love.

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:26.634
-And he, yeah.
-Was he worried

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:28.267
that you weren't going to pay
any attention to him

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:30.033
because you were also
in the movie?

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:31.634
-Because I was in the movie?
-As a director, he's like,

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:33.200
I'm worried that you
as a director

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.767
are going to pay
no attention to me.

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:36.968
-I thought that was a little,
maybe a little bit of a worry.

00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:38.968
But I have to say, like,
we didn't have playback

00:02:38.968 --> 00:02:40.567
while we were shooting,
we weren't able to look.

00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:42.334
-So you weren't watching.
You would shoot a scene,

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:43.534
and you wouldn't be able to go
and watch it, right?

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:45.734
-No, no, so in the edit room,
I was like,

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:49.667
"Who's that blonde like blob in
the movie with David Duchovny?"

00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:51.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:53.467
So he had nothing
to worry about.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:56.567
-He was telling us that you
shot in a working airport,

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:58.234
mostly at night
when nobody was there,

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:00.701
but you also had to face
the challenges of daytime

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:01.767
when there were
actually travelers.

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:04.400
-Yes, we had
real travelers who really

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:05.667
didn't care at all about us.

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:07.601
-Yeah.
This big apparatus,

00:03:07.601 --> 00:03:09.934
all this light,
all this -- all this commotion.

00:03:09.934 --> 00:03:11.133
And they didn't --
they didn't care much,

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:13.567
but it made us
have to be on our toes.

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:16.434
We did eight minute long takes,
which for a movie actor,

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:18.000
you just don't do that.
-Yeah.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.601
-So, and the great thing about
shooting in an airport at night

00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:23.000
is that, well, eventually
no one's there.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.133
-Yeah.
-So we just felt like

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.400
we had the place to ourselves.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.067
We made a movie
kind of in secret,

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:30.567
and now it's just like, weird
feeling putting a movie out.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:33.767
It's like sending
your first born child out,

00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:35.868
like, or your
or your oddest child out.

00:03:35.868 --> 00:03:37.367
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.634
And just saying, "You just go be
yourself, honey."

00:03:39.634 --> 00:03:41.200
-We thought you'd stay at home,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.868
but you are going
to go out in the world.

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:45.234
-Time to just go
out there and be yourself.

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:46.667
-This is your second film
you've directed.

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:48.834
Is there -- is there some
element of filmmaking

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:51.667
that you enjoy
the most that surprised you?

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:53.901
-Well, you're just in on it
with everybody,

00:03:53.901 --> 00:03:55.167
which is great
because you're working with

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.234
all these different kinds
of brands of artists,

00:03:57.234 --> 00:04:00.767
these, you know,
masters of light and sound,

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:03.868
and your senses become
very like, heightened,

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:06.133
you know, like it's
a heightened sensory experience

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:07.801
and you're in on it
with the other actors.

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:09.167
And a lot of times
you're just -- you just

00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:10.901
know your own process,
you know?

00:04:10.901 --> 00:04:12.968
But this -- this way,
you're just -- you're,

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:14.801
I don't know, you've just got
a secret with everybody.

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:17.467
-I've always been so impressed
when I stopped by sets,

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:18.868
when friends of mine
are directing a film,

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:21.367
there's just a thousand
choices a day.

00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:23.133
Like, that's the part
you underestimate

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.767
is you think a director
is just focused on the words

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:27.634
and these two great actors,

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:30.634
and then someone comes over
with like 30 spoons

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:32.033
and they're like,
"Which spoon?"

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:33.234
-And, yeah, you could --
-Yeah.

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:34.234
-You're right.

00:04:34.234 --> 00:04:35.868
And this was like
I directed --

00:04:35.868 --> 00:04:38.234
There I am, like, I --
I had to do so much,

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:40.267
I had to be so concentrated.

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.467
I had to really prepare,
prepare, prepare, prepare.

00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:44.234
I was there for
five weeks beforehand.

00:04:44.234 --> 00:04:46.033
And then really it was

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:48.667
so I could just not think
about it with David,

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:50.567
so I could just
kind of be loose.

00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:52.968
And my character
is like a, you know,

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:55.534
she's just a woo-woo,
very flaky person.

00:04:55.534 --> 00:04:58.767
And as a -- and as a director,
you have to really micromanage.

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.868
So I kind of -- every time
they said action,

00:05:00.868 --> 00:05:02.067
I was like, "Alright,
like, I'm just going

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:03.267
to relax a little bit.

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:04.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:07.467
Just be here with Mr. Duchovny."

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:09.601
-You dedicated this film
to Nora Ephron,

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:11.133
who obviously is a director
you worked

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:15.367
with quite famously
to great result.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:16.901
What did you learn
most from her?

00:05:16.901 --> 00:05:18.100
Because obviously
you've done films

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.634
with so many different
kinds of directors.

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:22.901
-From her,
I just learned the pure delight.

00:05:22.901 --> 00:05:25.067
She had pure delight
in moviemaking.

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:27.868
And the the thing about writing
and directing is that,

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:31.400
you know, the words come
to you in a -- it's so private.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.634
You know, your home, like,
it's 5:00 in the morning

00:05:33.634 --> 00:05:36.968
and you run the scene in your
head and you write it down,

00:05:36.968 --> 00:05:38.434
and then all of a sudden
you cut to the set

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:41.968
and there's 150 people who care
about bringing that to life

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:45.601
and have opinions about that,
and it's a joyful thing.

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:49.501
And she had like the joy of
giving this great dinner party

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:50.868
every time you were on.

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:52.334
The sets with her
were really fun.

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:53.734
-That's great.
-I heard Hugh Grant

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:55.434
say one time,
I didn't personally hear it,

00:05:55.434 --> 00:05:58.033
but I read that he said doing

00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:01.067
a comedy is like being a balloon
in a world of pins.

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:02.434
-Uh-huh.
-That's true.

00:06:02.434 --> 00:06:04.467
And you just don't want the set
to be a world of pins.

00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:06.300
-Yeah, that's a
good way of looking.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.167
You -- you --
It's trick or treating.

00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:11.667
It's It's Halloween,
it's New York City.

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:14.334
I actually have a son who's
going to be a vampire today.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:15.434
-Oh, you do?
-A five-year-old son

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:16.434
is going to be a vampire.

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:18.000
This is your son, Jack.
-Yes.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.868
-When he was six?
-He was about six years old.

00:06:19.868 --> 00:06:22.601
-And this is, obviously,
he has --

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:24.300
he comes
from show business stock.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.000
His makeup is that good?
-Yeah.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.434
He was so interested.
He was very --

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:28.834
-My five-year-old
looks like [bleep]

00:06:28.834 --> 00:06:30.834
compared to him.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.834 --> 00:06:32.868
I should know your son,
Jack Quaid, he's a great actor.

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:34.234
He's also been a guest
on the show.

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:35.601
I'm a big fan of his, we are.
-He's so funny.

00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:37.934
This -- he wanted --
he wanted to be Dracula,

00:06:37.934 --> 00:06:39.801
but then he told me
like he want --

00:06:39.801 --> 00:06:43.901
he had a name and he wanted
to be Count Jackula.

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:45.300
-Count Jackula?
-Yeah.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.767
-Yeah.
--I was like, no, man.

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:51.000
You just have to trust me
on this one.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.534
-They're already like
seven of those on the subway.

00:06:53.534 --> 00:06:54.934
-No, no.
-Yeah.

00:06:54.934 --> 00:06:57.133
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:58.534
-You don't --
and they don't like...

00:06:58.534 --> 00:07:01.033
-You don't do that.
[ Applause ]

00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:02.501
-Congratulations on the film.

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:04.033
It has just been so wonderful
having you here.

00:07:04.033 --> 00:07:05.100
Please come back soon.
-Thank you.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.868
-You guys, Meg Ryan.

00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:08.567
"What Happens Later"
is in theatres Friday.

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:10.033
We'll be right back
with Caitlin Collins.

